  "image_name": "403e561d3b3ded3c5b17a48bdd1053e8.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure gives an overview of bioelectronic interfacing using an organic electrochemical transistor (OECT) as a bridge between the body and an electronic device. On the left, a simplified human body with internal organs is linked to the device by two arrows that emphasize the desired balance: efficient transfer of biological information to electronics, while causing minimal disturbance to the biological environment. This summarizes the core goal of the interface.\n\nAt the center, the OECT is shown schematically with source, drain, gate, and an ion-containing channel. Charged species from the electrolyte appear to move between the gate region and the channel, illustrating how ionic biological signals can modulate electronic conduction in the transistor channel. Beneath the device, several representative organic semiconductor materials are shown by chemical structures, including PEDOT:PSS, BBL, and p(g2T-T), suggesting the kinds of mixed ionic-electronic conductors used for these interfaces.\n\nOn the right, the figure groups application spaces for bioelectronic interfaces: biomolecule-functionalized channels or gates, in vitro systems with 2D and 3D interfaces, skin-mounted devices on conformable substrates with electrolyte reservoirs, and in vivo uses requiring compatible materials and suitable form factors. Overall, the image presents OECTs as adaptable platforms for translating biological activity into readable electronic signals.",
